# A1: Allocating constructors directly in C is fine

There is nothing wrong with allocating constructors from compiled C code. ALO
just allocates lazily. Direct allocation is equivalent. If your constructor
emission segfaults, the bug is somewhere else in your code -- not in the
concept of constructor emission itself. You are most likely breaking some other
invariant (maybe double-reading a linear resource, or corrupting the heap
layout). Do not blame constructor emission. Debug the actual segfault.

# A2: Priority: strict forcing at MAT/SWI sites

Your MAT/SWI code already forces the scrutinee via aot_force_strict when it
sees DP/APP/ALO/REF tags. But then if the forced result STILL isn't a
constructor (C00..C16) or a NUM, it deopts. The millions of deopts you see
are happening because forcing isn't going far enough -- the scrutinee reduces
to something that needs MORE reduction steps to reach a constructor.

The fix is straightforward: after forcing, if the tag still isn't what you
expect, you need to keep reducing (call wnf on it). This is what the
interpreter does. The compiled code should do the same. The forced WHNF of
any well-typed scrutinee in a MAT/SWI WILL be a constructor or NUM
eventually -- if it isn't, you haven't reduced it enough.

Focus on making strict MAT/SWI forcing actually reach WHNF instead of doing
a partial force and giving up.

